course: dictionary
lesson: 14499
title: A formal word about money
level: primary-6
stars: 2
explain: Pecuniary is a formal word. It means to do with money. You will meet it mostly in law, business and exam writing, not in stories about dogs and football.
explain: Find the clue in the sentence: «Selling her old toys gave Amara a pecuniary gain of ten pounds.» Gain and pounds tell you that money is the topic, so pecuniary must mean to do with money.
explain: Check by swapping your meaning in: «Selling her old toys gave Amara a money gain of ten pounds.» It still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
fact: Pecuniary means to do with money.
fact: «Pecuniary gain» means getting money.
fact: «Pecuniary loss» means losing money.
fact: Pecuniary is a formal word, so it is most often found in law, business and exam writing.
fact: Financial is a close synonym for pecuniary.
exercise: «Selling her old toys gave Amara a pecuniary gain of ten pounds.» What does pecuniary mean here? | To do with the weather | To do with books | *To do with money | To do with animals
exercise: «The broken window caused the shop a pecuniary loss.» What is the loss? | *A loss of money | Harm to someone's feelings | A loss of a pet | A loss of sleep
exercise: Which word means the same as pecuniary? | Happy | Quiet | Tall | *Financial
test: Mum said the lottery win was a great pecuniary gain. What was gained? | *Money | A holiday | Friends | Sunshine
test: «The town had pecuniary problems, so it had no money for a new park.» What were the problems about? | Trees | *Money | Water | Dogs
exam: «The judge said there was no pecuniary motive for the crime.» What does this mean? | *Money was not the reason for the crime | The crime was about the weather | Money was the only reason for the crime | The crime was very loud
